<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/13th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">War of the Euboeote Succession</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1256">1256</a>–<a href="/years/1258">1258</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The War of the Euboeote Succession was fought from 1256 to 1258 between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-ii-of-villehardouin">William II of Villehardouin</a>, prince of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaea">Achaea</a>, and a coalition of the lords of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euboea">Euboea</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guy-i-de-la-roche">Guy I de la Roche</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>, over the succession to a lordship in Euboea. William's victory at Karydi in 1258 confirmed his supremacy in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/war-of-the-euboeote-succession.jpg" alt="War of the Euboeote Succession" width="320" height="252" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: William R. Shepherd · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:ShepherdByzempire1265.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fourth-crusade">Fourth Crusade</a> the strongest <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> lordship of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> was the Principality of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaea">Achaea</a>, which held the whole <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnese">Peloponnese</a>. William of Villehardouin, prince from 1246, sought to bring the other Latin states under his rule. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guy-i-de-la-roche">Guy I de la Roche</a>, Great Lord of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thebes">Thebes</a>, was already his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argo">Argos</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nafplio">Nauplia</a>, and William was suzerain of the three <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombard</a> baronies, or terzieri, of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euboea">Negroponte</a>, the medieval name of Euboea. The Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> also claimed overlordship of the island, where it had held trading rights since 1209.</p><p>In 1255 Carintana dalle Carceri, who held the northern triarchy of Oreos, died. Under a pact of the triarchs her heirs were dispossessed in favour of the surviving triarchs, Guglielmo of Verona and Narzotto dalle Carceri, and her relative Leone dalle Carceri appealed to William as their suzerain.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>According to Marino Sanudo, William imprisoned the two triarchs in early 1256 and occupied Oreos and their part of the city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euboea">Negroponte</a>, until the Venetian bailo Paolo Gradenigo drove his men out. On 14 June 1256 at Thebes the triarchs exchanged their <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassalage</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaea">Achaea</a> for that of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> and ceded it the citadel guarding the Euripus bridge. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guy-i-de-la-roche">Guy I de la Roche</a>, William de la <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hoffmann-la-roche">Roche</a>, Thomas II d'Autremencourt of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amfissa">Salona</a> and Ubertino Pallavicini of Bodonitsa joined them, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genoa">Genoa</a> and Othon de Cicon of Karystos backed William.</p><p>William's nephew Geoffrey of Briel retook <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euboea">Negroponte</a>, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marco-gradenigo">Marco Gradenigo</a> besieged it for thirteen months and forced its surrender in early 1258, and Geoffrey himself changed sides. William crossed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isthmus-of-corinth">Isthmus of Corinth</a> and in May or June 1258 defeated the coalition at the pass of Mount Karydi. Guy fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thebes">Thebes</a> and, at the pleas of its archbishop and William's nobles, was spared a siege on his pledge to appear before the Achaean High Court.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The High Court referred Guy's case to King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-ix-of-france">Louis IX of France</a>, who pardoned him and gave him the title of duke. Peace talks began on 6 August 1258, but William's capture at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pelagonia">Pelagonia</a> in 1259 delayed the final treaty until his release in 1262. It restored the status quo: the fortress of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euboea">Negroponte</a> was razed, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> kept its enlarged quarter in the city and its right to levy customs in Euboea.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “War of the Euboeote Succession” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Under William II Achaea was the leading military power of Latin Greece, and in the <b>War of the Euboeote Succession</b> he defeated Guy I de la Roche, lord of Athens, at the Battle of Karydi in 1258.</blockquote><a href="/empire/principality-of-achaea">Principality of Achaea · Military</a></li></ul></section>
